# Defect 314, 2026-10-07, the map beside its string. Symptom: a map released
# once more than it was held was freed twice, the runtime asking nothing: on
# this Mac the allocator's own trap at 4 and 32 entries (exit 133, no word of
# the runtime's), its abort at 1,000, and nothing at all at 1 (exit 0),
# measured at `dad2da47`; the Windows heap stopped it as heap corruption with
# nothing said. Cause: the map's release took a count of 0 to -1 in freed
# memory and released the block again, the array's check never written for
# it. Now the count is asked as an array's is, and the key descriptor, which
# a live map always has, is set to none before the block goes, so a release
# finding the allocator's word where the count was is refused too
# (`runtime/parts/map.c`).
#
# The map is the C's own, made through the runtime and released twice by a
# function of the program's own header: a map does not cross to C, and the
# compiler never releases one twice. AddressSanitizer names the read of the
# freed block.

extern "fixedbugs-314-a-map-released-twice-is-named.h"
    function release_twice(entries: i64)

function main()
    print("four entries, released twice")
    release_twice(entries: 4)
